OSKit Arch — TRISC CPU Primitives

Architecture-specific CPU operations for TRISC: interrupt
enable/disable and thread stack frame construction.

    module oskit.arch

Offset from saved SSP to the return-value register (r1)
in the context frame. Used by syscall_return to write the
result where restore_thread will pop it into r1.

The TRISC context frame is saved in boot.asm as:
USP, r6, r5, r4, r3, r2, r1, PC, PSR
(pushed in that order, so PSR is at lowest address).
SSP points at USP (bottom). r1 is at SSP + 6*8 = 48.

    val SYSCALL_RETURN_OFFSET = 48

Disable interrupts. Must be called before accessing
shared kernel state (run queues, thread table, etc.).

    arch_cli()
        asm("cli")

Enable interrupts. Called after kernel state updates
are complete, allowing the timer and device IRQs to fire.

    arch_sti()
        asm("sti")

Spinlock acquire. Spins using atomic CAS until the lock
is obtained, then disables interrupts. The lock word is 0
when free, 1 when held. r1 = lock address (from param).
Uses r2 (expected=0) and r3 (new=1) as scratch.

    arch_spinlock_acquire(lock: *i64)
        asm("ldi r3, 1\n_spin_acq\nldi r2, 0\ncas r2, r1, r3\nbne r2, r0, _spin_acq\ncli")

Spinlock release. Enables interrupts, then stores 0 to
the lock word (8-byte store). r1 = lock address (from param).

    arch_spinlock_release(lock: *i64)
        asm("sti\nstd r0, r1, r0")

Build a fake context frame on the supervisor stack so
that restore_thread in boot.asm can pop it and RTE into
the new thread. TRISC layout (9 fields, top to bottom):
PSR, PC, r1, r2, r3, r4, r5, r6, USP

    val STACK_FRAME_SIZE = 72        // 9 fields × 8 bytes (PSR, PC, r1-r6, USP)

Per-process kernel stack top. Updated by tss_set_rsp0 on
context switch. The trap handler reads this to switch to the
kernel stack before calling slow-path syscall handlers.

    var current_kstack_top: i64 = 0

    tss_set_rsp0(rsp0: i64)
        current_kstack_top = rsp0

    extern thread_exit: byte

    build_stack_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64) -> i64
        var sp = ssp
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 2                 // PSR: Mode=1 (supervisor), Ind=0 (interrupts on)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = entry_point       // PC
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 0                 // r1
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 0                 // r2
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 0                 // r3
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 0                 // r4
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 0                 // r5
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = i64(&thread_exit) // r6 = return address (exit trampoline)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = usp               // USP
        sp

TRISC build_user_stack_frame — same as build_stack_frame.
TRISC doesn't distinguish ring 0/3 in the context frame.

    build_user_stack_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64) -> i64 =
        build_stack_frame(entry_point, usp, ssp)

Set up the initial context frame for a new process. On TRISC,
the context frame lives on the process's SSP (because the hardware
pushes PC+PSR there on trap). Build in a temp buffer and copy
to the child's address space. Returns the SSP value for the thread.

    arch_setup_process_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64, kstack_top_val: i64, ptbr_val: int) -> i64
        var frame_buf: [72]byte
        val frame_ssp = i64(&frame_buf[0]) + STACK_FRAME_SIZE
        build_stack_frame(entry_point, usp, frame_ssp)
        vm_copy_to(ptbr_val, int(ssp) - STACK_FRAME_SIZE, &frame_buf[0], STACK_FRAME_SIZE)
        ssp - STACK_FRAME_SIZE
